<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Classical Greece</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 480–323 BCE</span> · Also known as Classical period of ancient Greece</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> is the period of ancient Greek history from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a> of the early fifth century BCE to the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 323 BCE. It saw the rivalry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, Athenian democracy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, together with the drama, philosophy, history writing, architecture and sculpture that later ages took as models.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/classical-greece.jpg" alt="Classical Greece" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikos Kitsakis · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Acropolis_of_Athens_as_seen_from_the_Pnyx_in_October_2025.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
